relative-uniqueness-f173f04e.symmetry_origin:
This formula is a direct consequence of Gauss's Law in integral form (
\oint \mathbf{E} \cdot d\mathbf{A} = \frac{Q_{enc}}{\varepsilon_0}
) when applied to an infinitesimally thin 'pillbox' Gaussian surface that straddles a charged interface. By taking the limit as the height of the pillbox approaches zero, the contributions from the side walls vanish, and only the normal components of the electric field (and thus the normal derivative of the potential) through the top and bottom faces remain, revealing the discontinuity directly related to the enclosed surface charge.
